libva-v4l2-rk3399 (1.0.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Encode latency: convert RGB32 uploads to NV12 with NEON inside
    vaPutImage, hand the VPP an NV12 frame by move instead of copying RGBA,
    add a scoped big-core affinity hint for the conversion, and copy the luma
    plane into the V4L2 buffer in one memcpy.  pixelflux 720p live encode goes
    from 24.2 ms to 17.4-17.9 ms (15.4 ms with the consumer pinned to A72).
  * Fix the image buffer allocator: calloc memory was released with delete and
    every 4K P010 vaCreateImage zeroed 25 MB.  Image buffers now use malloc/free
    and are not pre-zeroed.
  * Decoder setup uses 20 capture buffers instead of 24 (about 90 MB less at
    4K10) while covering FFmpeg's 20-surface pool.
  * Validation: 5 s 720p30 encode at 73.9-80.6 fps; 5 s 4K60 Main10 decode at
    50.5 fps ondemand / 54.2 fps with the performance governor; 720p HEVC at
    ~300 fps and still bit-exact.

 -- William Luke <126307463+WillXLuke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 20:20:00 +0800

libva-v4l2-rk3399 (1.0.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * HEVC Main and Main10 decode exposed through VA-API: map the VA picture
    and slice buffers to the modern stateless V4L2 controls, fill the DPB
    with capture-buffer timestamps, translate VA RefPicList[] (indices into
    ReferenceFrames[]) to V4L2 DPB indices, and pass the inline short-term
    RPS bit offset so rkvdec does not decode RPS bits as slice data.
  * Main10: expose P010 surfaces, negotiate them with FFmpeg, and unpack
    rkvdec's packed NV15 capture buffers in vaGetImage/vaDeriveImage.
    vaExportSurfaceHandle remains unimplemented for 10-bit.
  * Reduce the 4K buffer footprint (24 capture buffers, 4 MiB compressed
    frame buffers) and keep profile-aware SPS priming before REQBUFS.
  * Validation: 1950 frames of 3840x2160@59.94 Main10 (32.53 s) at 62.4 fps
    end-to-end with the performance governor (57.7-60.5 on ondemand); 390-frame
    P010 output bit-exact against software decode.

 -- William Luke <126307463+WillXLuke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 19:30:00 +0800

libva-v4l2-rk3399 (1.0.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Performance: upload RGB frames straight into the surface (derived image
    aliases the surface memory, removing an 8 MB copy per frame), and split the
    NV12 chroma planes with NEON. 1080p capture+encode went from ~21 to
    ~26 fps at 42 ms/frame on the same content.

 -- William Luke <126307463+WillXLuke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 18:10:00 +0800

libva-v4l2-rk3399 (1.0.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Validation pass: 4K H.264/HEVC decode, 1080p VP8/MPEG-2 decode, 1080p30
    H.264 encode.
  * Kernel patch: the VPU2 JPEG encoder tops out at 8176x8176 (511 macroblocks
    per row); the driver advertised 8192 and silently produced a 0x0 JPEG.

